description:
Lesson 3.03's ring carries fixed-size numbers. Real messages vary in size. Build a single-producer, single-consumer ring of **variable-size messages** inside one block of memory, as a game would use to stream events to an external tool through a file mapping.

```cpp
class MessageRing
{
public:
    static constexpr std::uint32_t MAGIC{ 0x474E4952 };      // "RING"
    static std::size_t bytesNeeded(std::uint32_t capacity);

    MessageRing(void* memory, std::uint32_t capacity, bool create);
    bool valid() const;
    bool send(std::span<const std::uint8_t> payload);
    std::optional<std::vector<std::uint8_t>> receive();
    std::uint64_t pending() const;
};
```

**Layout.** The block starts with a header, then `capacity` bytes of data area. `capacity` is a power of two and at least 64. The header holds the magic, the capacity, and two `std::atomic<std::uint64_t>` counters on separate cache lines: `head` (total bytes ever written to the data area) and `tail` (total bytes ever consumed). **bytesNeeded** returns the header's size plus `capacity`. With `create` false, the object reads the capacity from the header; **valid** checks the magic.

**Records.** A message is written at data position `head % capacity` as a 4-byte length followed by the payload, the whole record padded to a multiple of 8 bytes. Because every record starts at a multiple of 8, there are always at least 8 bytes before the end of the data area.

**Wrapping.** If a record does not fit in the space before the end of the data area, the producer writes a **wrap marker** (length `0xFFFFFFFF`) at the current position, counts the rest of the data area as used, and writes the record at the start. A record never straddles the end.

**send** returns false, changing nothing, if the payload is longer than `capacity / 2 - 4` bytes, or if there is not enough free space (`capacity - (head - tail)`) for the record plus any wrap padding. It writes the record **then** publishes the new `head` with release ordering.

**receive** returns `std::nullopt` when empty. It skips a wrap marker (moving `tail` to the start of the next lap), reads the record, and publishes the new `tail` with release ordering. **pending** is `head - tail`.

